## 6. Critical Task 2 — Final HTTP, HTTPS and FTP Result Audit

Inspect the actual implementations:

- `HttpEndpointTester`
- `FtpControlTester`
- `UrlParser`
- Result classification models.
- Error mapping.
- Scan coordinator.

Review every status mapping.

### 6.1 HTTP Classification

Do not map all HTTP error responses to one misleading status.

Use accurate classifications.

| HTTP result | Expected interpretation |
|---|---|
| 2xx | HTTP response successful |
| 3xx | Redirect |
| 401 | Authentication required |
| 403 | Access denied |
| 404 | Resource not found |
| Other 4xx | Client-side HTTP response |
| 5xx | Server-side HTTP response |
| DNS failure | Hostname resolution failed |
| Connection refused | Connection rejected |
| Timeout | Deadline exceeded |
| TLS failure | Secure connection failed |

All valid HTTP responses demonstrate some level of communication with an HTTP server.

They must not automatically be reported as network-unreachable.

An HTTP 404 must not be described as the same thing as a connection failure.

If the UI groups several cases under a broader `HTTP Responding` category, preserve the actual status code and explanatory detail.

### 6.2 FTP Classification

Review FTP status semantics.

A `220` greeting proves the FTP control service provided a successful greeting.

It does not prove:

- Authentication success.
- Data-channel availability.
- Passive-mode functionality.
- File availability.
- File download success.

Classify `421` as FTP service unavailable or temporarily unavailable, rather than incorrectly treating it as an HTTP-style access restriction.

Verify:

- Single-line greeting.
- Multiline greeting.
- Preliminary `120`.
- Subsequent `220`.
- `421`.
- Malformed response.
- Greeting timeout.
- Maximum line length.
- Maximum banner length.
- QUIT behavior.
- Cancellation and socket closure.

FTPS must remain explicitly unsupported unless a verified implementation exists.

Never downgrade FTPS to plain FTP.

### 6.3 HTTP Redirect Audit

Verify:

- Bounded redirect count.
- Loop detection.
- Correct handling of HEAD and GET.
- HTTPS-to-HTTP downgrade rejection.
- No credential forwarding across hosts.
- No unsupported scheme redirect.
- Proper response closure.

### 6.4 HTTP Byte-Bounded Fallback

Verify the existing HEAD and GET fallback.

A server may ignore the Range header.

Ensure that:

- The response body is consumed through a hard bounded reader.
- The byte limit is enforced independently of `Content-Length`.
- The remaining body is not buffered.
- The response is closed immediately when the limit is reached.
- The result correctly states that the HTTP endpoint responded even when its requested resource is restricted.

